<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!-- Harness input, and the third document in this tree that is never built into an
     image: it is SUBMITTED over the management API to a node already running
     `systems/qemu-x86_64/configuration.xml`, and what it proves is that a POLICY
     decides whether an ICMP error related to a live conversation may cross.

     IT DIFFERS FROM THE SHIPPED DOCUMENT BY ONE RULE. `probe-related` is added;
     every attribute of every other element, both existing rule ids, both actions
     and both destination ports are the shipped document's, so nothing about
     admission, the denying rule or the fallthrough moves under this submission.
     That one rule is the whole of the experiment.

     WHY THE EXPERIMENT NEEDS TWO DOCUMENTS. An ICMP error carries a copy of the
     datagram that provoked it, and the tracker relates it to a flow by
     corroborating that copy — the quoted source must be the party the error is
     addressed to, the five-tuple must name a flow the table holds, and the
     direction it implies must be one that flow has carried traffic in. What
     corroboration settles is where the error would GO. It must not settle whether
     it MAY, because the error is composed by whoever sent it, with a source
     address of their choosing, and delivered to an endpoint of a conversation
     somebody else opened.

     So the same error, on the same flow, is put to the filter under both
     documents:

       * under the shipped one, whose rules are both `protocol="udp"`, no rule is
         about it and the default deny refuses it — which is the posture
         everything else here is under;
       * under this one, `probe-related` admits it and it crosses.

     A document that named related traffic in neither case would leave "the policy
     denied it" and "the tracker never related it" looking alike, which is why the
     denial and the admission are one scenario and not two.

     `tracking="related"` IS THE WHOLE CRITERION. The rule states no ports —
     stating one on ICMP is refused before a document commits, an echo's
     identifier standing where a port would — and no `icmp-type`, because the
     three error types that relate are one class of thing to an operator and
     naming one of them would make the rule about a code rather than about the
     relationship. The management addressing is the shipped document's, because
     the client that submits this reaches the node at that address and would
     otherwise be cut off by its own submission. -->
<configuration>
    <interfaces>
        <interface id="dataplane-0" port="0" enabled="true"
                   mac="52:54:00:12:34:50" address="10.0.0.1" prefix-length="24"/>
        <interface id="dataplane-1" port="1" enabled="true"
                   mac="52:54:00:12:34:51" address="10.0.1.1" prefix-length="24"/>
    </interfaces>
    <neighbours>
        <neighbour id="endpoint-a" interface="dataplane-0"
                   address="10.0.0.2" mac="52:54:00:00:00:0a"/>
        <neighbour id="endpoint-b" interface="dataplane-1"
                   address="10.0.1.2" mac="52:54:00:00:00:0b"/>
    </neighbours>
    <rules>
        <rule id="probe-blocked" ingress="any" egress="any"
              source="any" destination="any" protocol="udp"
              source-port="any" destination-port="5001" icmp-type="any" tracking="any" action="drop"/>
        <rule id="probe-forward" ingress="any" egress="any"
              source="any" destination="any" protocol="udp"
              source-port="any" destination-port="5000" icmp-type="any" tracking="opening" action="accept"/>
        <rule id="probe-related" ingress="any" egress="any"
              source="any" destination="any" protocol="icmp"
              source-port="any" destination-port="any" icmp-type="any" tracking="related" action="accept"/>
    </rules>
    <management mac="52:54:00:12:34:52" address="10.0.2.15"
                prefix-length="24" enabled="true" gateway="10.0.2.2"/>
</configuration>
